I replaced the linear scan with a lazily built 65536-entry index table (message number → 1-based array index), so each lookup is one array access instead of scanning all MESGS entries; first-match semantics are preserved by only filling empty slots.

```cpp
const Profile::MESG* Profile::GetMesg(const FIT_UINT16 num)
{
    static FIT_UINT16 index[65536] = {};
    static bool initialized = false;

    if (!initialized)
    {
        for (int i = 0; i < MESGS; i++)
        {
            const FIT_UINT16 n = mesgs[i].num;
            if (index[n] == 0)
                index[n] = static_cast<FIT_UINT16>(i + 1);
        }
        initialized = true;
    }

    const FIT_UINT16 idx = index[num];
    return idx ? &mesgs[idx - 1] : NULL;
}
```

message.reasoning:
The function is a linear search over MESGS entries. MESGS is probably a few hundred. We can speed it up with a static lookup table built once, or binary search if sorted. But we can't add new files or change other functions. We can add a static local cache inside the function.

Best approach: build a static lookup table (array indexed by num, size 65536) lazily on first call. Since num is FIT_UINT16 (0..65535), a 65536-entry pointer/short array is 128KB-512KB. That's fine.

But we need to know the profile's mesgs array. It's a static member presumably. Let's write:

```cpp
const Profile::MESG* Profile::GetMesg(const FIT_UINT16 num)
{
    static FIT_UINT16 lookup[65536] = {};
    static bool init = false;
    if (!init)
    {
        for (int i = 0; i < MESGS; i++)
            lookup[mesgs[i].num] = (FIT_UINT16)(i + 1);
        init = true;
    }
    FIT_UINT16 idx = lookup[num];
    return idx ? &mesgs[idx - 1] : NULL;
}
```

Wait, but mesgs[i].num could be 0? FIT message numbers... mesh numbers start at 0 (file_id = 0). So storing i+1 avoids 0 ambiguity. Good.

But is mesgs a static member accessible? The function uses `mesgs[i]` directly, so yes it's accessible in this scope. It's `Profile::mesgs` presumably declared as static const MESG mesgs[MESGS]. Indexing works.

Caveat: MESGS is a compile-time constant. lookup array 65536 entries of FIT_UINT16 = 128KB static. Fine.

Need to make sure num range is uint16 so index valid. Yes.

Thread safety: no threads allowed, fine.

Also we could store pointers directly: static const MESG* lookup[65536]. That's 512KB on 64-bit. 128KB with uint16 better cache. Use uint16.

Actually the mesgs array might have MESGS > 65535? Unlikely.

Need to include? FIT_UINT16 already defined. No extra headers needed except maybe none. static initialization is fine.

